You unhook the rope from your pack and coil it once in your hands, thinking. The ledge is high, the drop far, and a rope would run out long before the bottom — fifty feet of hempen line against a forty-yard fall. You would be swinging on its end in the dark, no floor beneath you, and that is not a thing to do on a guess.

There is still the matter of finding a way down before you can trust the rope to anything. You could edge along the ledge and feel the wall with your hands, testing where the old miners cut their steps; or push the lantern's light farther out to read the far wall; or call down into the black and listen for what answers.

