You work along the ledge with your palm never leaving the wall, slow and deliberate, refusing the dark any trust it has not earned. You pause at nine feet or so and run your hands over the rock low and high, fingers reading the cold stone the way an old dwarf does — by the pattern of the cutting, not by sight alone.

And there it is. Past the patch of shattered crust, your fingers find what your eyes missed: the edge of the wall breaks inward, and a narrow stair begins, cut clean into the living rock. Old steps, their edges worn smooth by generations of boots before the seal went up, descending steeply along the chamber wall into the black. The first step is as solid as the mountain itself; you test it with your boot, and it does not shift.

The stair is no wider than a man, dropping away at a steep pitch, with only the cold wall on one side and open air on the other. The way down is here. It is long, and it is dark, and it waits below your lantern's little ring of light.

