You go down as an old miner taught you: boot first, weight second, hand never leaving the wall, the lantern held low enough that its light runs ahead of you down the steps and the open air stays on your shield side. Each step is tested and found solid. The stair is steep but true, and the worn centers of the treads speak of countless boots that came this way long ago.

The wall curves, and the black swallows the ledge behind you. Now there is nothing but the little globe of gold around your feet and the long, slow sound of your breathing, and the mountain thinking to itself in the dark.

Every dozen steps or so you stop and hold still, and the sound is there each time, clearer now and steady: a slow drawing in and out, deep, regular, unhurried — from somewhere below and ahead of you, as regular as a sleeping thing. It does not quicken, and it does not come to meet you.

You are perhaps a third of the way down, the chamber's floor still lost below, when the wall under your hand grows rougher — scraped, scored, exactly like the stone around the old seal — and your lantern finds the first of the marks on the stair itself: a long, pale groove worn up the center of the steps, as if something with claws had climbed this stair many, many times.

