It is old. The edges of the scoring are worn smooth and rounded, filled in places with dust, polished to a dull sheen where the stone has been ground down over years of use. Nothing fresh has cut this stone in a long, long time — not in a generation, and not in the few days since the miners vanished. Whatever made it passed this way when the workings still breathed, and it passed often enough to wear the rock.

The marks are wide, wider than a man's hand. Where a single foot-fall pressed deepest, you can trace the ghost of several claws side by side — three, perhaps four to a limb, set like a dog's great paw but larger than any hound that ever ran the mountain. The groove runs down as much as up; the thing used this stair both ways, as a road.

