You turn the pick over in the lantern light. The iron is clean of blood — no dark ring at the eye, no smears down the haft — only the grey bloom of dust. But the wood of the handle has been marked, long ago, in the way miners mark their tools so they come back from a work gang: a crude anvil scratched at the end of the grip, and under it two letters, cut deep and certain — K. H.

You tuck the tin cup into your pack, sling the pick, and move out along the trail.

At first the tracks are easy. Then, fifty paces out, the dust begins to fail, scraped thin by years of some slow current in the air, and the trail grows faint — a partial heel here, a scuffed toe there, and between them long bare stretches where the stone gives you nothing. But the men walked straight, and the glints lie dead ahead, so you hold the line and follow what remains.

You stop well short of the glints and raise the lantern a little, shield up before you. The light runs out across the chamber floor and finds them all at once: coins. Scores of them, scattered in a wide, shallow ring, copper and silver and the dull gleam of a few gold pieces, lying on the stone as if emptied from a man's pockets as he walked. Among them, a boot — a miner's boot, standing sole-down as if the man had simply stepped out of it — and beyond that, spaced out toward the glints, a second boot.

No body. No blood. Just the money lying scattered, and the boots left behind in a line, as though the three men had walked out of their own feet.

